#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078205 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d47c0304-a438-5444-9add-5e4f61acbbcd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and Another Term"}] :question/id #uuid "426f52d9-ad0e-5ef3-a545-78315ea02c0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-1514" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term $a_1 = 1$ and the $4$th term $a_4 = 8$, what is the index of the term $32$?" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that $a_4=8$, we can use the formula for the $n$th term to solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n8 &= 1 \\cdot r^{4 - 1} \\\\\n8 &= r^{3} \\\\\n2^{3} &= r^{3} \\\\\n2 &= r\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\na_{n} = 1 \\cdot 2^{n - 1}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for the value of $n$ that gives $a_n=32$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= 1 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n32 &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{5} &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n5 &= n - 1 \\\\\n6 &= n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n=6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078206 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa87d67-d9df-5bbb-b98e-00d72d16fe7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Particular Term When the First Term is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "e3fed3e8-9bc6-558d-a426-96006871a20d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1516" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has $a_2 = 24$, $a_5 = 3$ and $a_k = 12$, what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that $a_2=24$ and $a_5=3$, so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{n - m} &= \\frac{a_{n}}{a_{m}} \\\\\nr^{5 - 2} &= \\frac{a_{5}}{a_{2}} \\\\\nr^{5 - 2} &= \\frac{3}{24} \\\\\nr^{3} &= \\frac{1}{8} \\\\\nr^{3} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{3} \\\\\nr &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that $a_k=12$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{n - m} &= \\frac{a_{n}}{a_{m}} \\\\\nr^{k - 2} &= \\frac{a_{k}}{a_{2}} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{k - 2} &= \\frac{12}{24} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{k - 2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{k - 2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{1} \\\\\nk - 2 &= 1 \\\\\nk &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078306 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078436 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90216c0e-e4a5-5933-86e0-58d218939e29" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the Recursive Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "d7c0ca60-1915-5484-913a-835a1f8fa320" :question/math-academy-id "e-4240" :question/problem "What is the index of the term $16$ for the geometric sequence below?\n\n$a_{n+1} = 2 a_n \\quad a_1 = \\dfrac{1}{4},\\quad n\\geq 1$" :question/worked-solution "This geometric sequence is given as a recurrence relation, i.e.,\n\n$$\na_{n+1} = {\\color{blue}r} \\cdot a_n\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\na_1 = \\dfrac{1}{4}, \\quad {\\color{blue}r}=2\n$$\n\nWe're interested in finding $n$ such that $a_n = 16$. Using the formula for the $n$th term, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n16 &= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{4} &= \\frac{1}{2^{2}} \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{6} &= 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n6 = n - 1\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad n = 7\n$$\n\nTherefore, $16$ is the $7$th term of the sequence."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078307 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078437 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a82355f8-4b86-55c8-8686-1e4ea82e4464" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Term Given the First Term and the Common Ratio"}] :question/id #uuid "2da03f9b-c474-5b07-b4e8-bdf1dc3c2fd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-4241" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has the first term $a_1 = 5$, the common ratio $r = 2$, and the $n$th term $a_n = 640$, what is the value of $n$?" :question/worked-solution "To find the index, we substitute our known values of $a_1$, $r$ and $a_n$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n640 &= 5 \\cdot 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n\\frac{640}{5} &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n128 &= 2^{n - 1} \\\\\n2^{7} &= 2^{n - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the exponents gives:\n\n$$\n7 = n - 1\\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad n = 8\n$$\n\nWe can check this result by substituting $n = 8$ into the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} \\cdot r^{n - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 2^{8 - 1} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 2^{7} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot 128 \\\\\n&= 640✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaa87d67-d9df-5bbb-b98e-00d72d16fe7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Index of a Particular Term When the First Term is Unknown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4f9efab-c17e-5931-874b-5cb792746161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077463} {:answer/id #uuid "be4a1d02-6c11-53d8-bb2a-bbddaa5995c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077464} {:answer/id #uuid "c36b5c65-40b1-552d-862a-de2a63efc6f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077465} {:answer/id #uuid "f504c69c-388e-57d9-a607-08dc26fde645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186077466} {:answer/id #uuid "08c4a91e-aadd-53e9-b0dc-ff0807a0ad64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 1759